WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.018
Rebecca Clark could hardly wait to exit the small airplane.

2
00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:07.606
She got out, darted through the snow, and hugged her father.

3
00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.520
Rebecca was very excited to see him again.

4
00:00:10.521 --> 00:00:16.919
This was her first visit to the Arctic, where Dr. Clark had been working as a polar scientist for over three months.

5
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.852
Rebecca knew from her father’s research that the Arctic is the most northern part of the planet.

6
00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:26.310
Not many people live there because the weather is very cold most of the year.

7
00:00:26.311 --> 00:00:29.844
The Arctic has the longest winters and the shortest summers.

8
00:00:29.845 --> 00:00:33.076
It also has the longest days and the longest nights.

9
00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:40.074
In the summer, the sun shines almost all the time, and in the winter, the night can last up to six months.

10
00:00:40.075 --> 00:00:43.908
Rebecca's father and his team had brought lots of fresh food with them.

11
00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:52.988
This was because the Arctic soil, called permafrost, is permanently frozen, making it impossible for trees, vegetables, or fruit to grow.

12
00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:59.240
The scientists had enough supplies to last them, but there was nothing Rebecca's father wanted more than a visit from his daughter.

13
00:00:59.241 --> 00:01:04.474
He was eager to tell her about his Arctic adventures.

14
00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:07.623
They settled in his cabin and warmed up with some tea.

15
00:01:07.624 --> 00:01:12.867
"Dad, did you make it to the North Pole?" asked Rebecca.

16
00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:18.347
"Yes, but I didn't walk there; I flew over it in a special airplane," he said.

17
00:01:18.348 --> 00:01:22.799
Rebecca was curious. "Can you walk to the North Pole?"

18
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:29.735
"Yes," he replied, "but it is very hard to do, and many have died trying.

19
00:01:29.736 --> 00:01:34.419
As you know, Rebecca, the North Pole is a very difficult place to visit.

20
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:39.908
For centuries the North Pole remained one of the last unconquered places on Earth.

21
00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:48.120
People tried to see what was at the top of our planet, but they were unable to cross the many kilometers of ice and snow.

22
00:01:48.121 --> 00:01:54.681
In the 1500s, explorers from Holland and England were trying to find a new route to Asia,

23
00:01:54.682 --> 00:01:59.018
and they thought that going through the Arctic Ocean would shorten the way.

24
00:01:59.019 --> 00:02:05.122
A few explorers tried this route, but their ships got stuck in the ice.

25
00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:11.681
They returned home discouraged, and some explorers looked for other routes to the riches of Asia.

26
00:02:11.682 --> 00:02:20.488
Most explorers forgot about the Arctic. It wasn't until the late 1800s that the golden age of Arctic exploration began."

27
00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:25.163
"Why did people try again?" asked Rebecca.

28
00:02:25.164 --> 00:02:34.132
"Well, even though explorers were no longer looking for a passage to Asia, they were seeking personal glory," explained Dr. Clark.

29
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:43.855
"Navigating a route through the Arctic and conquering the North Pole would give any explorer fame and fortune and would bring prestige to his country.

30
00:02:43.856 --> 00:02:45.947
So the race was on."

31
00:02:45.948 --> 00:02:49.556
"What do you mean?" asked Rebecca.

32
00:02:49.557 --> 00:02:59.564
"In 1893 the Norwegian explorer Fridtjof Nansen was the first to break through the ice and get very close to the North Pole.

33
00:02:59.565 --> 00:03:08.443
His famous ship, called the Fram , was especially designed for traveling through thick sea

34
00:03:08.444 --> 00:03:17.488
ice. Nansen got as far as he could, and when the Fram couldn't push through the ice anymore, he set out to reach the North Pole on

35
00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:23.254
foot. He took one of his crewmen with him, and they proceeded on sleds pulled by dogs.

36
00:03:23.255 --> 00:03:27.686
Exhausted after many days, they could go no further.

37
00:03:27.687 --> 00:03:38.140
They left the Norwegian flag at 86°14' N, which was the most northern latitude any explorer had ever reached.

38
00:03:38.141 --> 00:03:43.753
Fridtjof Nansen became a hero, but the North Pole had not been conquered yet."

39
00:03:43.754 --> 00:03:48.844
"So who made it first?" Rebecca asked as she poured more tea.

40
00:03:48.845 --> 00:03:56.568
"This is debatable," her father answered. "Two explorers claimed to reach the pole first.

41
00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:05.178
Robert Peary, a brave American who was obsessed with exploring the North Pole, led numerous expeditions across the Arctic.

42
00:04:05.179 --> 00:04:09.137
He finally succeeded in his quest in 1909.

43
00:04:09.138 --> 00:04:14.741
Peary learned to adapt to life in the frozen north from the Inuit people.

44
00:04:14.742 --> 00:04:19.798
They are often called Eskimos and have lived in the Arctic for thousands of years.

45
00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:25.743
They taught Peary how to dress warmly with sealskin and accompanied him on his expeditions.

46
00:04:25.744 --> 00:04:30.356
Finally, on his last expedition, together with his most trusted crew member,

47
00:04:30.357 --> 00:04:37.699
Matthew Henson, and fifty Inuit helpers, Peary crossed the Arctic and reached the North Pole.

48
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:43.008
Upon his return to the United States, however, Peary learned some terrible news.

49
00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:52.086
Frederick Cook, another American explorer, was claiming that he had already reached the North Pole—one year before Peary.

50
00:04:52.087 --> 00:04:59.244
No one could prove which of the two explorers, if either of them, had actually reached the pole.

51
00:04:59.245 --> 00:05:09.158
It was tricky because back then there was no way of communicating with explorers so far north, and their expeditions could last a year or longer.

52
00:05:09.159 --> 00:05:14.200
Also, explorers had very primitive ways of figuring out their position.

53
00:05:14.201 --> 00:05:25.978
They used an instrument called a sextant to measure how far the sun rose above the horizon, which in turn told how far north or south they were.

54
00:05:25.979 --> 00:05:33.810
A sextant combines a telescope, a ruler, and a mirror, and requires skill to use accurately."

55
00:05:33.811 --> 00:05:36.836
"Then what happened?" asked Rebecca.

56
00:05:36.837 --> 00:05:42.551
"Both men insisted they were right, and a scandal erupted," replied Dr. Clark.

57
00:05:42.552 --> 00:05:48.893
"In the end, the National Geographic Society backed Peary's claim of reaching the North Pole.

58
00:05:48.894 --> 00:05:54.789
But even today, some still question whether Peary really succeeded."

59
00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:58.067
"Why do people doubt him?" asked Rebecca.

60
00:05:58.068 --> 00:06:04.648
"Robert Peary was a great explorer," explained her father, "but not a very good navigator.

61
00:06:04.649 --> 00:06:10.731
Some people thought he had read his instruments wrong when he claimed that he was at the North Pole."

62
00:06:10.732 --> 00:06:17.788
"How about you, Dad? What do you use when you go exploring?" Rebecca asked.

63
00:06:17.789 --> 00:06:24.415
"Today life in the Arctic is very different from what it was one hundred years ago," said her father.

64
00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:31.109
"We use airplanes, submarines, and special ships called icebreakers to visit the North Pole.

65
00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:35.448
Since Peary's trip, there have been hundreds of Arctic expeditions.

66
00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:40.516
We use computers and satellite telephones to keep in touch.

67
00:06:40.517 --> 00:06:49.359
Many scientists spend months in the Arctic doing tests, collecting data, and researching different scientific occurrences."

68
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.170
"Just like you, right, Dad?" Rebecca said.

69
00:06:53.171 --> 00:06:58.184
She looked out the window at the pale sun, still shining in the late Arctic evening.

70
00:06:58.185 --> 00:07:01.707
"And me," she added with a wide smile.

71
00:07:01.708 --> 00:07:11.656
"I’m going to be a polar scientist when I grow up too!"

